78 #edb/ref 17592186481647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481655 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "53237724-b18f-554e-b4b7-e3f73a8381db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481661 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481661 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481661 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481661 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481661 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481661 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481661 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481661 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481661 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bc9aaa6a-a4ef-548d-866f-aa031c7198d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481662 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481662 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481662 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481662 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481662 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481662 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481662 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481662 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481662 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e1fe577e-eb1d-5087-b60c-da2397f3533b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481667 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481667 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40217" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481667 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481667 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481667 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "887e0ea0-dd4d-5fcd-a5bc-286204a862b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481667 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,3\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11})≈{58.52}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481669 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481669 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481669 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481669 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481669 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a43e3223-e818-53e0-8311-b6df64e15405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481669 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=0[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=0[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{(0)(0)+(-1)(-1)+(0)(2)+(1)(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=0[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since we got the zero-vector as the projection, the vector $x$ must be  orthogonal to the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ Thus, the vector $x$ extends the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481670 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481670 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481670 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481670 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481670 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae3c05b7-f23f-59a0-b648-be66bb1e3e3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481670 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{12\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)}{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{12\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 0}{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The projection is the vector $x$ itself. This means that $x$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In other words, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481672 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481672 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81473" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481672 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481672 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481672 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eff4218e-d396-5a2a-a2a2-2a662de74575" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481672 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{4\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-4)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
